  "notes": "The home-studio writer-producer of 1990s confessional pop-rock, Gate-2 clear at 1990. 73–130 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and the signature records at 80–105. The method is the model: **demo-as-master** — the 1995 landmark \"was written and recorded as demos in his Encino home studio and largely kept, so the drums are programmed loops with sparse live overdubs\". That is why this producer's kit is tighter than the live band that plays his records: the grid stays hard while the velocity spreads. Three deltas are quoted: \"kick on 1 and the '&' of 2\", \"hard dry snare on 2&4\" (a loud accent with a very tight ghost), and \"a fill vocabulary of a **single crash** rather than rolls\", which puts the hat rolls at 0.02, the fills at every 16 bars and a crash lane in the percs. Harmony is \"riff-based modal vamps rather than functional progressions — two- or three-chord cycles (I–IV–V, i–VII–IV) with a droning tonic held by open strings or a keyboard pad; **the melody supplies the tension**\", so `maxChords` is 3, the harmonic rhythm leans on `vamp`, and the melody carries a low `chordToneBias` with a high density. His production \"treats the topline as a rhythmic instrument with long, syllable-dense phrases pushing across the barline\", and the demo-shaped forms give irregular 10- and 12-bar sections. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it medium-high and says the drum specifics are inferred. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 94.",
